6.text:
PUBLIC RESPONSE: {25 and 74}

The double-sestina end-word rotation (A=mountains … F=evening, perm 6-1-5-2-4-3) is already perfect in every supplied stanza; therefore the only lines that can be exchanged without breaking the visible skeleton are the two that both terminate on the same word “music.”  

Line 25 (“These forests eke … music”) is the canonical envoy opener attested in Sidney; line 74 (“Long since, alas … music”) is the generic line that belongs at the head of the fifth stanza. Swapping exactly those two restores both the historical text and the required three-line envoy (valleys–music–evening) while leaving the rotation untouched everywhere else. No other pair satisfies both constraints simultaneously.

15.text:
PUBLIC RESPONSE: {25 and 74}

The supplied text already obeys the canonical double-sestina rotation (mountains/valleys/forests/music/morning/evening) in every one of the twelve six-line stanzas; therefore any transposition that would break that skeleton is impossible. The only two lines that share the identical terminal word “music” are line 25 (“These forests eke … music”) and line 74 (“Long since, alas … music”).  

Line 25 is the unique line that belongs at the head of the three-line envoi (where Sidney’s original envoy opens with exactly those words); line 74 is the generic “Long since …” line that belongs at the start of the fifth stanza. Exchanging precisely those two lines restores both the historically attested envoy and the rhetorical progression of the poem while leaving every terminal word unchanged. No other candidate pair satisfies both the formal rotation and the textual history simultaneously.

