13.text:
PUBLIC RESPONSE: {13 and 19}

The double-sestina terminal-word skeleton is already flawless through stanza 12, so the anomaly cannot be diagnosed by end-word placement alone. What stands out instead is the duplicated syntactic frame: both line 13 and line 19 open with the identical formula “I, that was once …” followed by an immediate pivot to a former state. Every other paired recollection in the poem varies its opening construction (compare the “I, that was once free burgess” and “I, that was once delighted” stanzas with the later “Meseems I see” and “I wish to fire” turns). This exact repetition violates the expected principle of progressive rhetorical variation that the sestina form demands between the two speakers.  

Swapping 13 and 19 restores the alternating motif progression, lets each speaker’s recollection land in a fresh syntactic register, and allows the subsequent refrain words to echo with greater emotional force. In contrast, any transposition involving the envoy leaves this opening-motif clash untouched and merely rearranges lines whose terminal words already satisfy the rotation. The result is that the poem’s rhetorical architecture feels smoother and more consistent once 13 and 19 exchange places, which is the minimal change required by the form’s deeper design.

